+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: the server pushes each revision as it commits, giving subsecond updates without a realtime vendor or its recurring fees. Five-second HTTPS polling and a hosted realtime service were considered and rejected (see Key Design Decisions).
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+This choice shapes the three unbuilt components. The change store keeps a replayable revision history so a socket can resume where a client left off. Delivery owns socket sessions — authentication, connection capacity, and reliable publication to connected clients. Browser sync owns reconnect and catch-up: on reconnect, the browser resumes from its last seen revision rather than refetching the whole board.
 
